-- margins.psk — the course GPT's logits and decisions, for `make margins`
-- (ε from the margins, D118).
--
-- The model is 05_gpt.psk's, imported: that file is not edited, and an
-- import brings modules and
-- constants and not functions, so the token type is written here again,
-- as experiments/gpt_grad.psk does. The functions keep 05_gpt.psk's names
-- (`GPT.apply`, `Block.apply`, …), so a realization `make curve` solved on
-- its `loss` types the same sites here; its `loss/…` sites name nothing
-- here and are left out.
--
-- The margin, the top logit minus the second, is not written as an entry:
-- it needs a maximum over the vocabulary, and `max` is not implemented
-- (E2012). experiments/margins.py takes it from the exact logits that
-- `logits` prints at 128 bits and 30 digits, as the difference of the two
-- largest, exact to the digits printed. `decisions` is the realization's own
-- choice, `argmax` on its logits: the greatest index among equal maxima,
-- as the reference defines it.
--
-- The key: outside a training run `dropout`'s mask is all ones (the
-- `training` chapter), so no key changes a value here; `--seed 0` is passed
-- everywhere all the same.
import "../corpus/nlp/05_gpt.psk" as g

dim B
dim T <= 1024

type Tokens = idx[g.V][batch: B, seq: T]

fn logits(θ : params g.GPT, k : key, batch : (Tokens, Tokens)) -> ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, vocab: g.V] uses Rand =
  let (x, _) = batch
  g.GPT.apply(θ, x, k)

fn decisions(θ : params g.GPT, k : key, batch : (Tokens, Tokens)) -> idx[g.V][batch: B, seq: T] uses Rand =
  let (x, _) = batch
  argmax(g.GPT.apply(θ, x, k))
